          "parts": [
            {
              "text": "When contesting a will or bringing a **Family Provision Application (FPA)** under the *Succession Act 1981* (Qld) in Brisbane, engaging a firm that specifically handles **succession law and estate litigation** is crucial. In Queensland, estate litigation involves distinct procedural rules and strict deadlines.\n\nIndependent legal directories (such as *Doyle’s Guide for Leading Wills & Estates Litigation Law Firms in Queensland*) and the local legal profession consistently recognise the following Brisbane firms for estate disputes, will challenges, and FPAs:\n\n---\n\n### 1. Dedicated Boutiques & Estate Specialists\nThese firms focus almost exclusively on wills, succession law, and estate disputes:\n\n* **Cornford-Scott Lawyers** \n  * *Overview:* Frequently ranked as a top-tier succession law practice in Queensland. Angela Cornford-Scott and Prue Poole are preeminent accredited specialists who regularly handle complex will challenges, testamentary capacity disputes, and high-value FPAs.\n* **The Estate Lawyers** \n  * *Overview:* A Brisbane-based boutique firm dedicated purely to estate litigation, will disputes, and estate administration issues. Because they do not engage in general law, their resources are focused entirely on contested estates.\n* **de Groots Wills and Estate Lawyers** \n  * *Overview:* One of Australia’s pioneering succession law-only practices. Founded by Dr. John de Groot (co-author of the seminal text *Family Provision in Australia*), they have extensive experience representing both applicants challenging a will and executors defending an estate.\n* **Resolve Estate Law**\n  * *Overview:* Known for handling estate litigation and disputes with a particular emphasis on early dispute resolution and mediation, alongside formal Supreme Court litigation when necessary.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Leading Mid-Tier & Full-Service Firms (Litigation Teams)\nThese firms house dedicated, highly regarded estate dispute and private client divisions:\n\n* **McInnes Wilson Lawyers**\n  * *Overview:* Widely considered a premier firm for estate litigation in Queensland. Their team (including specialists like Fred Smith) routinely appears in the Supreme Court of Queensland for contentious probate matters and large-scale FPAs.\n* **Thynne + Macartney**\n  * *Overview:* One of Brisbane’s oldest and most respected independent firms. Their estate litigation group (led by Ashleigh Poole and Vicky Martin) represents executors, beneficiaries, and claimants in contested estates and dependency disputes.\n* **Mitchells Solicitors**\n  * *Overview:* Led by Bryan Mitchell (an Accredited Specialist in Succession Law), this firm has a strong focus on contentious estate disputes, elder abuse recovery, and family provision claims.\n* **Mullins Lawyers**\n  * *Overview:* Features a top-tier private client team (led by Michael Klatt) with deep expertise in defending estates against FPA claims and navigating multi-beneficiary inheritance conflicts.\n* **McCullough Robertson & Cooper Grace Ward**\n  * *Overview:* Major commercial practices in Brisbane with specialised succession and estate dispute departments, particularly well-suited for estates involving complex family trusts, business assets, or farming properties.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Plaintiff & Conditional (\"No Win, No Fee\") Options\nContesting a will can be expensive. If upfront legal fees are an obstacle, several firms assess claims for deferred payment or conditional fee structures (subject to eligibility criteria):\n\n* **Attwood Marshall Lawyers** (Brisbane CBD / Gold Coast)\n  * *Overview:* A prominent regional firm with a substantial dedicated estate litigation team. They routinely act in FPAs and often provide conditional fee arrangements for claimants with strong cases.\n* **Roche Legal**\n  * *Overview:* A Brisbane firm focusing specifically on contested wills and family provision claims on a \"No Win, No Fee\" basis for qualifying claims.\n* **Turner Freeman Lawyers & Maurice Blackburn**\n  * *Overview:* Large national plaintiff firms with Brisbane offices that operate dedicated estate litigation departments and frequently offer conditional fee agreements for excluded dependents.\n\n---\n\n### Key Queensland Deadlines to Keep in Mind\nIf you are planning to challenge a will or file a Family Provision Application in Queensland under the *Succession Act 1981*:\n1. **6-Month Notice:** You must give written notice of your intention to make a claim to the executor/personal representative within **6 months** of the deceased’s date of death.\n2. **9-Month Court Filing:** Formal court proceedings (originating application and supporting affidavit) must be filed in the Supreme or District Court within **9 months** of the date of death. \n\n*Missing these deadlines makes it significantly harder to pursue a claim, as you would need the court's leave to apply out of time.*",
